,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":11.520913353,"data":"  → VINCULUM:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":11.520918692,"data":"     The first delivery audit (Round 1) had 1/9 correct claims. This audit (Round 2) has 2/6 correct + 2 new failure modes (FABRICATED, HALF-TRUTH). The vinculum deletion test is working — each round exposes more overstatement. But the failure MODES are evolving, not resolving.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":11.520924491,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":11.520928325,"data":"  → ERDOS:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":11.52093227,"data":"     Same pattern as the sieve: the parametric identity (keyword matching) solves 100% of the trivial corridor (finding 'any weapons'), but the hard corridor (plan-specific requirements) remains unsolved. The real problem is never the easy search — it's the constraint matching.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":11.520937077,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":11.520940729,"data":"  → CHROMA CASCADE:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":11.520944682,"data":"     CP6 (real PBR) is mod9=4 GREEN — the only genuinely correct delivery. CP4 (wrong genre) is mod9=0 INFRA — self-destructive, actively harmful (wrong assets committed). CP5 (partial match) is mod9=3 YELLOW — caution, works partially but overclaimed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":11.520949607,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":11.520953926,"data":"  → MYCELIAL HYPHAE:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":11.520958639,"data":"     The dependency chain CP6→CP7→CP1-5 means the ONE correct delivery (CP6 materials) is the root hypha. Fix CP6 properly and CP7 (crafting) becomes viable. The other CPs are leaf nodes that can be regenerated.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":11.520964538,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":11.520968542,"data":"  → BUILDING VERBS:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":11.520972459,"data":"     EXTRUDE (x2): Double down on CP6 wiring — it's the only verified win. BEVEL (x3): NEVER download before verifying plan match. PAINT (x7): The 5 lessons learned above should become a reusable pre-download checklist.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":11.520977194,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":11.520980953,"data":"  → FRACTYPE:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":11.520985728,"data":"     730 files → ~100 plan-usable = 7.3x noise. The first-pass download strategy generated 86% waste. A pre-filter step would reduce this to near-zero waste.\n"}
